=3D"style-module-scss-module__nbagBW__learnContent"><header><h1>Introductio=
n to Sliding Windows</h1></header><h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>The sliding window technique is a subset of the two-pointer pattern, as =
it uses two pointers (generally left and right) to define the bounds of a "=
window" in iterable data structures like arrays. The window defines a subco=
mponent of the data structure (e.g., a subarray or substring), and it <em>s=
lides</em> across the data structure unidirectionally, typically searching =
for a subcomponent that meets a certain requirement.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"The image represents a sliding window concept often used in algo=
rithms.  It shows a sequence of elements represented by dots enclosed in sq=
uare brackets `[ . . . . . . ]`. A light-blue rectangular area highlights a=
 subset of these elements, labeled 'window'.  Two rectangular boxes, labele=
d 'left' and 'right,' are positioned above the sequence.  A downward-pointi=
ng arrow connects 'left' to the leftmost dot within the window, and a simil=
ar arrow connects 'right' to the rightmost dot within the window.  A light-=
blue upward-pointing arrow labeled 'window' indicates that this window can =
move along the sequence.  The arrangement visually demonstrates how the win=
dow's position is controlled by the 'left' and 'right' pointers, allowing i=
t to slide across the data sequence, processing a fixed-size subset at a ti=
me." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"244" height=3D"81" decoding=3D"async" data-n=
img=3D"1" style=3D"color:transparent" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/=
courses/coding-patterns/sliding-windows/introduction-to-sliding-windows/ima=
ge-05-00-1-6W2RRYSN.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div></fig=
ure>
<p>Sliding windows are particularly valuable in scenarios where algorithms =
might otherwise rely on using two nested loops to search through all possib=
le subcomponents to find an answer, resulting in an <span class=3D"katex"><=
span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/Math=
ML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi=
><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=
=3D"application/x-tex">O(n^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span c=
lass=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=
=3D"strut" style=3D"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><span cl=
ass=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span clas=
s=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n<=
/span><span class=3D"msupsub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span class=3D"vlist-=
r"><span class=3D"vlist" style=3D"height:0.8141em"><span style=3D"top:-3.06=
3em;margin-right:0.05em"><span class=3D"pstrut" style=3D"height:2.7em"></sp=
an><span class=3D"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class=3D"mord mtig=
ht">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=3D=
"mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time complexity, or worse. When usin=
g a sliding window, the subcomponent(s) we=E2=80=99re looking for can usual=
ly be found in <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xml=
ns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo st=
retchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annot=
ation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(n)</annotation></semantics></math></=
span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><=
span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><sp=
an class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span=
 class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span clas=
s=3D"mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time, in comparison. But before =
discussing how they work, let=E2=80=99s establish some terminology:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>To <strong>expand</strong> the window is to advance the right pointer:</=
p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"Image represents a visual explanation of an array expansion oper=
ation.  The left side shows an initial state with a light-blue rounded rect=
angle representing an array containing several black dots (elements).  Abov=
e this array are two gray rectangular boxes labeled 'left' and 'right,' eac=
h with a downward arrow pointing to the corresponding end of the array.  A =
gap with additional dots indicates the array's continuation beyond the visi=
ble portion. A thick black arrow labeled 'expand' connects the initial arra=
y to a modified array on the right. This expanded array is also a light-blu=
