ots, but now the 'left' and 'right' boxes are orange.  Dashed orange arrows=
 indicate that the 'left' and 'right' elements have been moved to the leftm=
ost and rightmost positions, respectively, causing a shift in the other ele=
ments.  The brackets '[' and ']' on both sides visually enclose the data st=
ructure, emphasizing its array-like nature.  The overall diagram illustrate=
s how elements are shifted within the data structure when a 'slide' operati=
on is performed, with new elements potentially added at the beginning and e=
nd." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"540" height=3D"54" decoding=3D"async" data-n=
img=3D"1" style=3D"color:transparent" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/=
courses/coding-patterns/sliding-windows/introduction-to-sliding-windows/ima=
ge-05-00-4-W7LLYWFO.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div></fig=
ure>
<p>Note that sliding is equivalent to expanding and shrinking the window at=
 the same time.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Now, let=E2=80=99s break down the two main types of sliding window algor=
ithms:</p>
<ol>
<li>Fixed sliding window.</li>
<li>Dynamic sliding window.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id=3D"fixed-sliding-window">Fixed Sliding Window</h3>
<p>A fixed sliding window maintains a specific length as it slides across a=
 data structure.</p>
