sliding-windows/image-05-00-6-JR24WU57.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoK=
Y1oV5c"></div></figure>
<p>Generally, dynamic sliding windows can be applied to problems that ask u=
s to <strong>find the longest or shortest subcomponent that satisfies a con=
dition</strong> (for example, a condition could be that the numbers in the =
window must be greater than 10).</p>
<p>When finding the longest subcomponent, the dynamics of expanding and shr=
inking are typically as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li>If a window satisfies the condition, expand it to see if we can find a =
longer window that also meets the condition.</li>
<li>If the condition is violated, shrink the window until the condition is =
met again.</li>
</ul>
